Name: Thinking, Fast and Slow
Author: Daniel Kahneman

Written by Nobel Prize-winning psychologist Daniel Kahneman, this book explores the two systems that drive the way we think: System 1, which is fast, intuitive, and emotional, and System 2, which is slower, more deliberate, and more logical. Kahneman draws on decades of research to reveal the cognitive biases and heuristics that shape our judgments and decisions, often without our awareness.

Recommended?
Yes. This is one of the most important and comprehensive books in behavioral psychology and cognitive science. It is an essential read for anyone studying decision-making, behavioral economics, or cognitive biases. The writing is accessible enough for casual readers but rigorous enough to serve as a reference for academic work. If you are only going to read one book on how the human mind works, this would be a strong candidate.
